Emulate the Windows D3DSWAPEFFECT_DISCARD contract, opt-in per game.

DXVK deliberately preserves the app-visible back buffer across Present when
the swap chain has a single back buffer -- its own comment a few lines above
this hunk says so ("Games with a buffer count of 1 rely on the contents of the
previous frame still being there"). Windows drivers do the opposite for
D3DSWAPEFFECT_DISCARD: the surface handed back is undefined, and in practice
blank.

Mass Effect 2's full-screen Scaleform screens (New Game difficulty, in-game
Options) issue no D3DCLEAR_TARGET at all -- a WINEDEBUG=+d3d9 capture of those
frames shows the back buffer as the render target for the whole frame with
only two D3DCLEAR_STENCIL calls -- and draw every row with alpha blending. With
the contents preserved, each frame composites onto the accumulated history of
the previous ones: the row-selection highlight leaves one solid green bar per
visited row and the slide-in animation leaves doubled labels. Everything opaque
in the same frame looks correct, which is why the surrounding art is fine.

wined3d preserves the back buffer too (PROTON_USE_WINED3D=1 corrupts
identically), so this is not DXVK-specific behaviour and not a Vulkan or RADV
issue; it is the wine-side back-buffer lifetime differing from Windows.

Gated behind DXVK_D3D9_DISCARD_BACKBUFFER=1 so only the game that needs it
pays the clear, and so the DLL stays a drop-in replacement everywhere else.

--- a/src/d3d9/d3d9_swapchain.cpp	2026-07-30 06:55:12.973028065 +0200
+++ b/src/d3d9/d3d9_swapchain.cpp	2026-07-30 06:55:12.974028049 +0200
@@ -189,6 +189,19 @@
       UpdateWindowedRefreshRate();
       UpdateTargetFrameRate(presentInterval);
       PresentImage(presentInterval);
+
+      // Emulate the Windows DISCARD contract for apps that rely on it: after
+      // Present the back buffer comes back blank instead of keeping the frame
+      // that was just shown. Opt-in, because DXVK's default of preserving the
+      // contents is what other single-back-buffer games depend on.
+      static const bool discardBackBuffer =
+        env::getEnvVar("DXVK_D3D9_DISCARD_BACKBUFFER") == "1";
+
+      if (unlikely(discardBackBuffer)
+       && m_presentParams.SwapEffect == D3DSWAPEFFECT_DISCARD
+       && !m_backBuffers.empty())
+        m_parent->ColorFill(m_backBuffers[0].ptr(), nullptr, 0);
+
       return D3D_OK;
     } catch (const DxvkError& e) {
       Logger::err(e.message());
